  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM MDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nHigh confidence in warm and dry weather continues through Saturday, \nbut a major pattern change is increasingly likely beginning around \nSunday. \n\nBroad ridging will continue to dominate the western US through the \nend of the week. Ensembles are in pretty good agreement showing the \nstrength of the ridge moderating just slightly on Wednesday and \nThursday, so forecast highs will be just a few degrees cooler \nthan today and tomorrow. Temperatures will be closest to records\nagain at Laramie and Rawlins, as the core of the ridge will \nremain to our west. While humidity will remain low and lead to \nelevated fire weather conditions, winds are expected to remain \nlight through this period. Therefore, critical fire weather \nconditions are unlikely. The ridge will start to amplify again \non Friday into Saturday as its axis shifts rapidly eastward \ntowards the central CONUS. 700-mb temperatures will remain \naround the climatological 90th percentile for this period. With \nthe ridge moving east, flow aloft will turn more to the \nsouthwest, opening the door to increased wind speeds especially \nin Carbon, Albany, and Converse counties. While high winds are \nunlikely (10-20% chance) this may increase fire weather concerns\nfor Friday and Saturday. In addition, a few ensemble members \nshow some disorganized vorticity meandering through our area \nunder the ridge during this period. Combined with the southwest \nflow increasing mid to upper level moisture, we may see isolated\nhigh- based showers returning to the forecast by the end of the\nweek. Current chances for measurable precipitation remain slim.\n\n\nMajor changes are increasingly likely beginning around Sunday. While \nconfidence is high that something will change, there is low \nconfidence in what the new weather pattern will look like. Ensembles \nare unanimously showing some type of powerful Pacific trough \napproaching the area Sunday or Monday, but are in very poor \nagreement on exactly how this moves through the area. The LREF suite \nis split nearly 50-50 into two clusters. In one cluster, the Pacific \ntrough moves through more quickly, and as an open wave. This would \nlead to a dramatic cool down on Sunday or Monday with higher chances \nfor widespread freezing temperatures. Overall QPF expectations \nare lower in this scenario (widespread 0.1 to 0.4\"), but the \nchance for snow falling in more populated areas would be higher.\nThe other cluster of ensembles depicts the upper level low \ndiving further south and closing off over the western US. The \nstorm moves through much more slowly, leading to significantly \nwarmer temperatures Sunday and Monday and a delayed cool down \nafter the end of the operational forecast period. In this \nscenario, overall QPF expectations look considerably higher \n(widespread 0.4 to 0.8\", but the chance for snow would be lower \ndue to warmer temperatures when precipitation is falling. One \nother interesting tidbit is that ECMWF AIFS and GEFS AI members \ntend to strongly prefer the faster, drier, and colder former \nscenario. This will be an interesting event to watch for the \nperformance of these relatively new guidance tools.",
